\details{
\subsection{The representations}{

With variables \eqn{X_1, \ldots, X_p}, evaluated at
\eqn{x_1, \ldots, x_p}:
\itemize{
\item The CDF is \eqn{P(X_1 \le x_1, \ldots, X_p \le x_p)}.
\item The survival function is \eqn{P(X_1 > x_1, \ldots, X_p > x_p)}: every
variable exceeding its value. This is not one minus the CDF, which is
the probability that \emph{at least one} exceeds its value. The two agree
only for a single variable. For the probability of any other event,
such as \verb{x <= 1, y > 3}, see \code{\link[=prob]{prob()}}.
\item The density is the joint density, for a continuous distribution.
\item The PMF is \eqn{P(X_1 = x_1, \ldots, X_p = x_p)}, for a discrete one.
}

The vectors are recycled to a common length (in the manner of
\code{\link[vctrs:vec_recycle]{vctrs::vec_recycle_common()}}), and the output has that length: element
\code{i} evaluates the representation at the point whose coordinates are the
\code{i}th element of each vector.
}

\subsection{Conditioning with \code{given}}{

\code{given} names the variables whose values are known, and the
representation is then about the rest of them. Arguments stay in the same
order either way: you still pass a value for every variable, and \code{given}
says which of those values are known rather than evaluated at. For a
bivariate distribution of \code{x} and \code{y}:
\itemize{
\item \code{eval_bi_density(d, x, y, given = "x")} is the density of \code{y} at \code{y},
given that the first variable equals \code{x}.
\item \code{eval_bi_cdf(d, x, y, given = "x")} is \eqn{P(Y \le y \mid X = x)}.
\item \code{eval_bi_survival(d, x, y, given = "y")} is
\eqn{P(X > x \mid Y = y)}.
}

The variables in \code{given} are the ones held at their values, sitting on the
right of the bar in \eqn{P(\cdot \mid \cdot)}. The word was chosen because
that is how the bar is read aloud.

Conditioning is on the variables \emph{equalling} their values. To condition on
an event such as \eqn{X > x}, use \code{\link[=prob]{prob()}}:
\code{prob(d, y > 3, given = x > 1)}.
}

\subsection{Which ones are available}{

A distribution states what it can, and the rest is worked out from it.
The CDF and survival function are each worked out from the other. The PMF
of a continuous distribution is zero. A distribution on finitely many
points is worked out by listing the points.

A conditional density or PMF is the joint one divided by that of the
\code{given} variables, which comes from \code{\link[=marginal]{marginal()}}. A conditional CDF or
survival function comes from the distribution's own \code{conditional}
property, if it states one (the multivariate Normal does); otherwise from
listing the points of a finite distribution; otherwise, for a continuous
distribution with a single variable left over, by integrating the
density.
}
}
